  "body": "# Get help\nSummary\n\n\nBase UI Input (which renders Field.Control) triggers React state updates on each keystroke (setDirty, setFilled), causing the input component/subtree to re-render continuously while typing. This happens even when the input is used in an uncontrolled way via React Hook Form register.\n\nThis makes Base UI Input hard to use in performance-sensitive forms, because RHF\u2019s usual \u201cuncontrolled input = minimal rerenders\u201d benefit is lost.\n\nExpected behavior\n\n\nWhen using an uncontrolled input (no value prop, only ref/onChange/onBlur from RHF register), typing should not force React re-renders of the input subtree on every keystroke unless the consumer explicitly opts into controlled state or subscriptions.\n\nActual behavior\n\n\nTyping into Base UI Input triggers re-renders on every keystroke.\n\n## Ask a question\n\nRequest / questions\n\n1. Is there an intended way to use Base UI Input in a mode that avoids per-keystroke React state updates (and therefore avoids re-rendering the control)?\n\n2. Would you consider an opt-out for tracking dirty/filled/focused state (e.g. trackDirty, trackFilled, trackFocus) for integrations where an external form library owns state/validation?\n\n3. Alternatively, could Field.Control avoid updating React state on each input event and instead update some of this state on blur/commit, or compute it from DOM without setting state?\n",
